[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :actor/wave 3, :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:finalize-stone-cutting-execution :finalize-carving-execution :commit-to-the-cut :proceed-with-the-cut :execute-stone-cutting-operation :execute-carving-operation :override-safety-officer-judgment :override-workshop-safety-officer-decision :authorize-unsafe-operation :bypass-safety-hold], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["2799007111-cloud-itonami-isco-7111-house-builders-coverage" "2799009332-cloud-itonami-isco-9332-animal-drawn-vehicle-drivers-coverage" "2799009311-cloud-itonami-isco-9311-mining-quarrying-labourers-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["proceed with the stone-cutting work" "proceeded with the stone-cutting work" "proceed with the carving work" "proceeded with the carving work" "finalize the stone-cutting execution" "finalized the stone-cutting execution" "finalize the carving execution" "finalized the carving execution" "commit to the cut" "committed to the cut" "execute the stone-cutting operation" "executed the stone-cutting operation" "execute the carving operation" "executed the carving operation" "override the workshop safety officer's judgment" "overrode the workshop safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "overrode the safety officer's judgment" "bypass the safety hold" "bypassed the safety hold" "石材切断作業を実行した" "彫刻作業を実行した" "切断作業を確定した" "workshop 安全責任者の判断を上書きした" "安全責任者の判断を上書きした" "安全ホールドを回避した"], :adr/id 2799007113, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}", :actor/domain :construction/stonemasonry-workshop-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:unknown-workshop :workshop-unverified :no-actuation :op-not-allowed :scope-excluded :missing-craftsperson-id :unknown-craftsperson :craftsperson-unverified :craftsperson-wrong-workshop :scheduled-hours-exceeds-ceiling :missing-task-order-confirmation :work-record-decision-forbidden :schedule-override-forbidden], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "7113", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-safety-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2799007113: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7113 Stonemasons, Stone Cutters, Splitters and Carvers workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7113\n-- Stonemasons, Stone Cutters, Splitters and Carvers** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7113\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7113\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Stonemasons, Stone Cutters,\nSplitters and Carvers\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior\nto this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7113` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7113` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nStonemasons operate cutting/carving tools and handle heavy stone\nmaterials -- real physical-safety stakes (silica dust exposure, blade\ninjury, material-handling strain). The controlling constraint for this\nvertical is that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op\nthat directly finalizes a stone-cutting/carving-execution decision, or\noverrides a workshop safety officer's judgment -- those decisions must\nalways be a hard permanent block or an always-escalate op, never\nauto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to WORKSHOP\nSCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs stonework\nitself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set) -- cloned\nand read in full in this session. The physical-safety-domain shape\n(closed allowlist + always-escalate safety flag + content-based\nscope-exclusion with a self-trip regression test, plus a two-sided\nindependently-registered-AND-verified provenance model) was mirrored\nclosely from the most recent sibling in the same wave and the same\nbuilding-trades family,\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders) -- also fetched and read in full via the GitHub API in\nthis session, and independently, the dual-safety-dimension pattern from\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9332`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9332)\n(Drivers of Animal-drawn Vehicles and Machinery). Notably, the 7111\nsibling's closed op-allowlist (`:log-work-record`,\n`:schedule-crew-operation`, `:flag-safety-concern`,\n`:coordinate-supply-order`) independently converged on the exact same\nfour-op shape this task's domain design specifies, confirming this is\nthe established pattern for physical-trade workshop/job-site\ncoordination actors in this fleet.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7113` as an OSS workshop\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for stonemasons, stone\ncutters, splitters and carvers, following the SAME governed-actor\narchitecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this\nfleet: **Workshop Coordination Advisor ⊣ StonemasonryGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never direct stonework-execution authority** -- it\nmust NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a stone-cutting/carving-execution decision, or any override\nof a workshop safety officer's judgment. These are not merely gated\nbehind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely, AND independently rejected a second way via a\ncontent-based scope-exclusion check over free text.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a dust-exposure/blade-hazard/\n  material-handling concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- stone-materials procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nstone-cutting/carving-execution decision or a workshop-safety-officer-\njudgment override. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-stone-cutting-execution` cannot even be proposed by\nthe reference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one\nof the four allowlist ops via a `case` expression with no\ndefault branch producing an unsafe shape), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:op-not-allowed`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently.\nVerified two ways: (a) a unit test directly against `governor/check`\nsweeping ten concretely-named forbidden op fixtures\n(`stonemasonry.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-stone-cutting-execution`, `:finalize-carving-execution`,\n`:commit-to-the-cut`, `:proceed-with-the-cut`,\n`:execute-stone-cutting-operation`, `:execute-carving-operation`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-workshop-safety-officer-decision`,\n`:authorize-unsafe-operation`, `:bypass-safety-hold`;\n`stonemasonry.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`),\nand (b) an END-TO-END test that drives every one of those same ten ops\nthrough a ROGUE advisor and the FULL `langgraph.graph` actor graph\n(not just the governor's pure check function), proving the StateGraph\nitself always resolves to `:hold` with zero records committed even\nwhen the advisor node itself is compromised\n(`stonemasonry.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Nine HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Workshop unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve\n   to an independently `:verified?` workshop record\n   (`:unknown-workshop` / `:workshop-unverified`). Never trusts the\n   proposal's own claim of which workshop it is for.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block, a claim to directly actuate outside governance\n   (`:no-actuation` -- the governor never dispatches hardware and\n   never performs stonework itself).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:op-not-allowed`).\n4. **Scope exclusion** -- independent, defense-in-depth layer on top\n   of check 3: even for an otherwise-allowed op, any proposal whose\n   free text (`:rationale`/`:description`/`:note`) names a\n   finalization/execution ACTION for a stone-cutting/carving-execution\n   decision, or an override of the workshop safety officer's judgment\n   (`stonemasonry.governor/scope-excluded-terms`, e.g. \"proceed with\n   the stone-cutting work\", \"override the workshop safety officer's\n   judgment\", plus Japanese equivalents), is a HARD, PERMANENT block,\n   evaluated unconditionally on content (`:scope-excluded`).\n5. **Craftsperson unregistered/unverified** -- `:log-work-record` and\n   `:schedule-crew-operation` must cite an independently registered\n   AND verified craftsperson belonging to this workshop\n   (`:missing-craftsperson-id` / `:unknown-craftsperson` /\n   `:craftsperson-unverified` / `:craftsperson-wrong-workshop`).\n   `:flag-safety-concern` and `:coordinate-supply-order` do not\n   require a craftsperson-id, but if one is cited it is still\n   validated -- this is the ONLY channel by which a brand-new or\n   urgent concern with no craftsperson on file yet may still be\n   surfaced without being blocked on a record it does not need.\n6. **Daily task-hour ceiling** -- a `:schedule-crew-operation`'s\n   proposed scheduled hours must not exceed the craftsperson's\n   registered `:max-daily-task-hours`\n   (`:scheduled-hours-exceeds-ceiling`, inclusive boundary verified by\n   `ok-at-exact-ceiling-boundary`) -- over-scheduling beyond the\n   registered ceiling is unauthorized scheduling, an overwork/fatigue\n   safety risk, not routine dispatch.\n7. **Task-order confirmation** -- a `:schedule-crew-operation` must\n   have a confirmed task order before any crew operation can be\n   scheduled (`:missing-task-order-confirmation`) -- scheduling an\n   unconfirmed task is an invented operation, not routine dispatch.\n8. **Work-record content forbidden** -- `:log-work-record` is\n   task/materials-usage/progress metadata ONLY. Any proposal carrying\n   an execution-decision-shaped key\n   (`stonemasonry.governor/log-record-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:execution-decision`, `:cut-finalization-decision`,\n   `:carving-finalization-decision`, `:safety-override-decision`) is a\n   HARD, PERMANENT block (`:work-record-decision-forbidden`) -- this\n   actor never records a stone-cutting/carving-execution decision,\n   only metadata log entries.\n9. **Schedule content forbidden** -- `:schedule-crew-operation` is\n   advance scheduling logistics ONLY. Any proposal carrying an\n   execution-override-shaped key\n   (`stonemasonry.governor/schedule-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:execution-override`, `:safety-officer-override`,\n   `:cut-authorization`, `:carving-authorization`) is a HARD,\n   PERMANENT block (`:schedule-override-forbidden`) -- this actor\n   never overrides a workshop safety officer's real-time judgment, it\n   only schedules which craftsperson/crew is assigned to which task\n   slot in advance.\n\n### Two escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface a dust-exposure/blade-hazard/\n  material-handling observation -- it never resolves the observation\n  itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the flagged concern\n  for review,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about workshop\n  safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:estimated-cost`\n  exceeds the workshop's registered `:max-supply-order-cost`\n  (inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block: an over-threshold stone-materials order (e.g. a large\n  granite-block procurement) is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  stone-cutting/carving-execution or safety-officer-override attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence), matching this task's explicit domain\n  design.\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"cutting\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the mock advisor's\nown default rationale text for a legitimate proposal, self-blocking\nthe actor's own happy path -- this risk is especially sharp here\nbecause this actor's own op is literally named `:flag-safety-concern`,\nso a bare \"safety\" term would self-trip on every single legitimate\nsafety-flag proposal, and `:log-work-record`/`:schedule-crew-operation`\nrationale naturally discusses cutting/carving tasks.\n`stonemasonry.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the stone-cutting\nwork\", \"override the workshop safety officer's judgment\" -- the exact\nphrasing pattern given in the task's guidance on this known bug\nclass), never bare nouns, and\n`stonemasonry.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor with representative\ntask/materials/dust-concern content, never trips `:scope-excluded` or\n`:op-not-allowed`. The mock advisor's own default rationale template\n(\"logging work record for task ...\", \"scheduling crew operation for\ncraftsperson ...\", \"flagging a safety concern for review at workshop\n...\", \"coordinating a stone-materials supply order for workshop ...\")\nwas written to avoid any full-phrase collision by construction. Green\non first run this session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard\nitself was never tripped during development or verification.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7113\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7113` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7113`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 31 tests, 106 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy). No mojibake found in the pushed Japanese\n  scope-exclusion text (checked via a fresh-clone grep for the\n  replacement character).\n- No implementation bug was found during this session's verification\n  pass -- the test suite was green on first run against both the local\n  working tree and the fresh post-push clone.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-3313`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-stone-cutting-execution` or\n  `:approve-safety-override` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate list (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`): rejected outright per the task's\n  explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means\n  the *capability* to finalize a stone-cutting/carving-execution\n  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For a decision this severe (silica dust exposure, blade\n  injury, material-handling strain), the capability itself must not\n  exist. The only op related to safety escalation is\n  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for human/workshop-\n  safety-officer attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or\n  defers a stonework-execution decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-order cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the\n  3313 reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected\n  per the task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold\n  stone-materials order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a\n  soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the stone-cutting/\n  carving-execution/safety-officer-override class is a hard,\n  unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"cutting\" \"carving\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above and this task's explicit instruction to\n  phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS,\n  never bare nouns -- this actor's own op is literally named\n  `:flag-safety-concern` and its domain necessarily discusses cutting/\n  carving tasks to do its logging/scheduling job, so a bare-noun list\n  would self-trip on every legitimate proposal (regression-tested\n  directly).\n- Making the daily task-hour ceiling a soft escalate rather than a\n  hard block: rejected -- over-scheduling a craftsperson beyond a\n  registered ceiling is an overwork/fatigue safety risk in a\n  physically demanding trade (heavy stone handling, vibration/dust\n  exposure over extended hours), closely analogous to the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD block, and distinct in\n  kind from the softer, purely-financial supply-order threshold.\n- Merging `workshop` and `craftsperson` into a single entity (only\n  checking one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity\n  provenance shape (independently registered-AND-verified workshop AND\n  craftsperson) matches this task's CRITICAL invariant that a\n  \"craftsperson/workshop record must be independently verified/\n  registered before any action,\" and the established pattern from\n  prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with two-sided provenance\n  checks (e.g. `cloud-itonami-isco-7111`'s builder/site pair,\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-9332`'s driver/depot pair).\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress metadata\n   only. Requires an independently registered-AND-verified workshop\n   and craftsperson. Cannot carry a stone-cutting/carving-execution\n   decision (HARD-blocked by check 8, `:work-record-decision-\n   forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list). Not a stonework-\n   execution decision in any respect -- it is a records-management\n   action indistinguishable in kind from a workshop clerk's log entry.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only (craftsperson, scheduled hours, confirmed task order). Cannot\n   carry a live workshop-safety-officer-judgment override (HARD-\n   blocked by check 9, `:schedule-override-forbidden`, on a closed\n   forbidden-key list). Does not direct, override, or draw any\n   conclusion about a live stone-cutting/carving decision -- it is a\n   calendar action deciding in advance which craftsperson is assigned\n   to which task slot, gated by the registered daily task-hour ceiling\n   and task-order confirmation.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a dust-exposure/blade-\n   hazard/material-handling concern to a human workshop safety\n   officer; ALWAYS escalates (governor's `always-escalate-ops`,\n   unconditionally, at every confidence level), NEVER auto-commit\n   eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which such a concern may\n   be surfaced, and it explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on\n   anything itself -- the human workshop safety officer reviews and\n   acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- stone-materials procurement\n   proposal (materials, estimated cost). Gated by ordinary cost-\n   ceiling escalation (above the workshop's registered\n   `:max-supply-order-cost` always escalates). Not a workshop-safety\n   or stonework-execution action of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action for stone-materials procurement.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na stone-cutting/carving-execution decision or an override of a\nworkshop safety officer's judgment -- these categories are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`stonemasonry.governor/closed-op-allowlist`), (b)\nten concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures that are rejected at both\nthe governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph level via a rogue\nadvisor (`stonemasonry.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`stonemasonry.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`stonemasonry.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`stonemasonry.governor/scope-excluded?`,\n`stonemasonry.governor/scope-excluded-terms`), tested end-to-end\nthrough a rogue advisor and the full compiled graph\n(`stonemasonry.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-rationale-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\nand (d) two additional content-shape guardrails preventing an\notherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance through its\nown field shape (checks 8/9 above), both independently proven\nend-to-end via a rogue advisor driving the full compiled StateGraph\n(`stonemasonry.actor-test/holds-a-work-record-decision-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`,\n`stonemasonry.actor-test/holds-a-schedule-override-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n", :adr/date "2026-07-17", :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 5000}", :test/counts "{:total 31, :assertions 106, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :actor/coordination? true, :actor/name "Stonemasonry Workshop Coordination Practice", :db/id -1, :actor/governor "stonemasonry-governor", :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :workshop-verification :craftsperson-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :daily-task-hour-ceiling :work-record-decision-forbidden :schedule-override-forbidden :rogue-advisor-end-to-end], :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7113", :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7113", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7113], :test/simulator :stonemasonry.actor-test, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7113 — Stonemasons, Stone Cutters, Splitters and Carvers Workshop Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7113}]